mfd: palmas: hold the interrupt across system suspend

Every resume from suspend to RAM produces a burst of

    dummy 0-0049: Failed to read IRQ status: -13

a few hundred to several thousand lines long.  The machine does come back,
so this is noise rather than a hang, but it is a real interrupt storm and
it lands in the middle of the resume path.

The palmas node is a wakeup source, so the i2c core registers the chip's
interrupt as the device's wake interrupt and dpm_suspend_noirq() arms it.
suspend_device_irq() leaves an armed wakeup interrupt enabled and unmasked
on purpose, which is what lets the PMIC wake the system.

On the way back, dpm_resume_noirq() calls resume_device_irqs(), which
clears IRQD_WAKEUP_ARMED and re-enables the line.  Runtime PM for the i2c
controller, however, is not restored until pm_runtime_enable() in
device_resume_early(), one phase later.  In that window every transfer on
the bus fails: omap_i2c_xfer_common() calls pm_runtime_get_sync(), which
returns -EACCES while runtime PM is disabled.

The palmas interrupt is level triggered and its handler is threaded with
IRQF_ONESHOT.  regmap_irq_thread() cannot read the status registers, so it
cannot ack the interrupt, so it returns IRQ_NONE, so irq_finalize_oneshot()
unmasks a line that is still asserted, and the whole thing repeats.  It
stops only when device_resume_early() reaches the i2c controller.

Measured on a DragonBox Pyra (OMAP5432, palmas at 0-0048): a single RTC
alarm, one nested interrupt, produced 233 deliveries of the parent
interrupt and 231 of these messages.

Disable the interrupt for the duration of the suspend and re-enable it in
the resume callback, which runs after device_resume_early() has restored
runtime PM on the bus.  disable_irq() here is lazy: the chip has no
irq_disable() callback and the descriptor is not marked unlazy, so nothing
is masked at the WUGEN until the interrupt actually arrives, and the chip
can still wake the system.  The delivery that does the waking is latched
pending and replayed by enable_irq(), so no event is lost.

With this applied, an RTC-alarm wake on the same machine delivers the parent
interrupt twice instead of 233 times and logs none of these messages.  Both
suspend to idle and suspend to RAM still complete and resume.

--- a/drivers/mfd/palmas.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/palmas.c
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <linux/mfd/core.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/palmas.h>
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm.h>
 
 static const struct regmap_config palmas_regmap_config[PALMAS_NUM_CLIENTS] = {
 	{
@@ -729,10 +730,40 @@
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(i2c, palmas_i2c_id);
 
+static int __maybe_unused palmas_i2c_suspend(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct palmas *palmas = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	/*
+	 * The i2c controller this chip hangs off is unusable between
+	 * resume_device_irqs() and device_resume_early(). Keep the
+	 * interrupt held off over that window, or the handler spins
+	 * failing to read, and so failing to ack, a level interrupt.
+	 */
+	if (palmas->irq)
+		disable_irq(palmas->irq);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int __maybe_unused palmas_i2c_resume(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct palmas *palmas = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	if (palmas->irq)
+		enable_irq(palmas->irq);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(palmas_pm_ops, palmas_i2c_suspend,
+			 palmas_i2c_resume);
+
 static struct i2c_driver palmas_i2c_driver = {
 	.driver = {
 		   .name = "palmas",
 		   .of_match_table = of_palmas_match_tbl,
+		   .pm = &palmas_pm_ops,
 	},
 	.probe = palmas_i2c_probe,
 	.remove = palmas_i2c_remove,
